@task
Title: Security review write-up
Status: Done
Owner: Subbu
@endtask

The VS Code extension had a path traversal issue. The paste folder setting is window scoped, which means a repository can set it in its own .vscode settings file. It was being joined with the document path and handed straight to the file writing code, so cloning someone else's project and pasting an image could write files anywhere on disk.

Subbu asked for a fix the same day. The first attempt compared paths as strings and that turned out to be insufficient: a repository can commit a symlink, so a folder called img can pass every string check and still resolve to somewhere outside the workspace. Two independent reviewers flagged it. The final fix walks each path segment and refuses any that is a symbolic link. That was verified against a planted symlink rather than argued about.

Two smaller items were hardened at the same time. The Linux clipboard path was interpolating the temp directory into a shell command, and the Windows path was doing the same inside a double quoted PowerShell string, which expands subshells. Both now pass the path as an argument instead. Neither was exploitable without control of the environment, so these were defensive.

Workspace trust was also pinned explicitly. The default was already safe but it was implicit, and the extension writes files and reads the clipboard.

Separately, include expansion turned out to be bounded by depth but not by total node count. Cycle detection tracks the active path so diamonds are legal, which is correct, but it means four includes per file at fourteen levels deep is 268 million nodes from under 1.5 kilobytes on disk. A total expansion ceiling was added and the specification now requires one.

